{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "247234568",
    "title": "04.4.1 Product Hierarchy",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/247234568",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "This page defines the SFCC product hierarchy for Zimmermann: **Master → Variant Group → Variant**. It also includes example Zimmermann-style data to show how the model should be represented before sample imports, integrations, and storefront rendering begin.\n\nPurpose\n=======\n\nThe product hierarchy is the foundation of the SFCC catalogue model. It determines how products are stored, how shoppers select colour and size, how data is imported from source systems, and how downstream integrations and feeds are built.\n\nFor Zimmermann, the expected hierarchy is:\n\n> **Master product → Variant Group → Variant SKU**\n\nThis structure is especially useful for fashion products where a single style exists in multiple colours, and each colour then exists in multiple sizes.\n\nHierarchy overview\n==================\n\n| Level | Primary purpose | Typical shopper meaning | Typical examples of data held at this level |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Master** | Represents the overall style | The product family shown on PLP and PDP | Product name, style code, long description, shared media |\n| **Variant Group** | Represents a variation value grouping, typically colour | The colour a shopper selects on the PDP | Colour, colour code, colour-specific images, swatch data, colour-level search/filter attributes |\n| **Variant** | Represents the purchasable SKU | The exact size and colour combination added to cart | SKU, size, size code, inventory, price, barcode, orderable status |\n\nHow the 3 levels work together\n==============================\n\nThe three levels serve different business and storefront needs:\n\n* **Master** is the parent style record. It gives SFCC a single product identity for the style.\n* **Variant Group** sits between master and variant to group all SKUs for one colourway.\n* **Variant** is the sellable item. It is the level used for stock, price, and size selection.\n\nA simple rule of thumb: if the shopper can only **view** it, it is often master or variant-group data; if the shopper can actually **buy** it, it is variant data.\n\nVisual structure\n================\n\nwide760Master Product\n└── Variant Group: Colour A\n├── Variant SKU: Size 0\n├── Variant SKU: Size 1\n└── Variant SKU: Size 2\n└── Variant Group: Colour B\n├── Variant SKU: Size 0\n├── Variant SKU: Size 1\n└── Variant SKU: Size 2\n\nIn practice, a shopper experiences this as one product style, then chooses a colour, then chooses a size.\n\nZimmermann practical example\n============================\n\nTo clarify this further, here is a practical example:\n\n* **Style Code**: `8635DSS261`\n* **Variant SKU pattern**: `8635DSS261-BLJB-0P`\n* **Example product name**: `Aster Fit & Flare Dress`\n* **Example colour value**: `Blue Jacobean`\n\nUsing that pattern, the hierarchy would look like this:\n\n| Hierarchy Level | ID example | Example display value | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Master** | `8635DSS261` | Aster Fit & Flare Dress | One style record shared across all colours and sizes |\n| **Variant Group** | `8635DSS261-BLJB` | Blue Jacobean | Colour-level grouping for all Navy Jacobean sizes |\n| **Variant** | `8635DSS261-BLJB-0P` | Blue Jacobean / Size 0 | Purchasable SKU |\n| **Variant** | `8635DSS261-BLJB-1P` | Blue Jacobean / Size 1 | Purchasable SKU |\n| **Variant** | `8635DSS261-BLJB-2P` | Blue Jacobean / Size 2 | Purchasable SKU |\n| **Variant Group** | `8635DSS261-BLK` | Black | Second colour-level grouping |\n| **Variant** | `8635DSS261-BLK-0` | Black / Size 0 | Purchasable SKU |\n| **Variant** | `8635DSS261-BLK-1` | Black / Size 1 | Purchasable SKU |\n| **Variant** | `8635DSS261-BLK-2` | Black / Size 2 | Purchasable SKU |\n\nnoted42916e59656\n\nThe exact size format may vary by product type and source data. The important rule is that the **variant** remains the unique sellable combination of style, colour, and size.\n\nThe exact size format may vary by product type and source data. The important rule is that the **variant** remains the unique sellable combination of style, colour, and size.\n\nWhy this model matters\n======================\n\nStorefront experience\n---------------------\n\n* **PLP** usually surfaces the style at master level\n* **PDP** lets the shopper choose colour first, then size\n* **Swatches** map to variant groups\n* **Add to cart** always uses the variant SKU\n\nSearch and merchandising\n------------------------\n\n* Search results can show one style instead of many duplicated SKUs\n* Colour can still be searchable and filterable\n* Merchandisers can control colour presentation without duplicating style data\n\nIntegration and feeds\n---------------------\n\n* MuleSoft can map source data cleanly into the correct hierarchy level\n* SKU construction follows the agreed style + colour + size pattern\n* Feed exports can include both style-level and SKU-level data as needed\n\nRules and assumptions\n=====================\n\n* Every variant must belong to one variant group and one master product.\n* Every variant group must belong to one master product.\n* Master products are not directly purchasable.\n* Variant groups are grouping records, not sellable SKUs.\n* Variants are the only purchasable records.\n* Colour is expected to be the primary variation grouped at the middle level for Zimmermann.",
      "format": "markdown"
    }
  }
}"}

